bevy-horizon: skip the setup of indirect draws with no instances before Turing

For mesa-switch (https://github.com/danfromtico/mesa-switch, by danfromtico and NaGaa95; MIT)
at 606441c9, after 0001-0011 (it adds a switch to 0008's). scripts/build_nvk.sh applies it;
this text before the first "diff --git" line is ignored by git apply.

Without drawIndirectCount, which NVK has only from Turing, Bevy's GPU culling still issues every
batch's indirect draw and writes a culled batch's instance count as 0 (bevy_pbr's
build_indirect_params.wgsl). So most of a pass's draws can be empty: Skate 3's late prepass
issues about 1,780 draws and draws next to nothing, yet takes 0.49 ms, and across perf8-perf15
its time grows by about 274 ns a draw. NVK's macros for Maxwell (NVK_MME_DRAW_INDIRECT and
NVK_MME_DRAW_INDEXED_INDIRECT) set each draw up whether or not it has instances: in Mesa's MME
simulator, an empty indexed draw takes 54 instructions and 16 methods, 4 of them constant
buffer updates.

This adds two macros, NVK_MME_DRAW_INDIRECT_SKIP_EMPTY and
NVK_MME_DRAW_INDEXED_INDIRECT_SKIP_EMPTY, that read an empty draw's parameters and branch past
its setup, and that store the draw count to scratch once rather than at each draw. In the
simulator an empty draw takes 14 instructions and no methods, and a draw with instances one
instruction and one method fewer than before. Every draw with instances still sets all of its
state before it begins: for a mix of empty and non-empty draws, with padding between draws and
with multiview, the methods' values and constant buffer 0 at each BEGIN are the same as with
the upstream macros. After the call they are the last draw with instances' values, as if the
empty draws had not been issued. The upstream macros are unchanged; with the two new ones the
34 macros take 1,201 instruction words, within the 2,048 that nouveau's GL driver allows
(nvc0_graph_set_macro).

Pre-Turing indirect draws use the new macros unless NVK_SWITCH_TUNE_SKIP_EMPTY_DRAWS, a new
0008 switch (knob 5), is 0, which picks the upstream ones, to time one against the other in
one launch. Device-generated commands (nvk_cmd_indirect.c) keep the upstream macro.

